<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martín de Goiti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1574">1574</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martín de Goiti was a Basque conquistador who served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> in the Spanish conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. In 1570 he led an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> that clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-sulayman">Rajah Sulayman</a> and burned the town, and in 1571 he defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> and Kapampangan fleet at Bangkusay. He was killed in 1574 during the attack of the Chinese corsair Limahong on Manila.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Goiti was one of the soldiers of the Spanish voyage of exploration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> and the Pacific in 1565, which sought gold, spice and places to settle. Its aim was a route to the islands where the earlier Spanish expeditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a> in 1521 and Ruy López de Villalobos in 1543 had landed. On the orders of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> he later led the expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Spaniards reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> on 8 May 1570 and camped on the shores of Manila Bay for several weeks while making alliances with the local peoples. On 24 May hostility broke out. The Spaniards defeated most of the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-sulayman">Rajah Sulayman</a>, marched to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasig-river">Pasig River</a> and on 6 June 1570 occupied the settlements of Manila and burned them. Goiti also fought the chieftains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-matanda">Rajah Matanda</a> and Rajah Lakandula.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">Guerrilla</a> fighting followed for about ten months. The Spaniards fortified themselves and built the barracks of Fort Santiago, which became their outpost for trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and gained control of the settlements on 24 June 1571, after López de Legazpi arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, which became the permanent capital of the Spanish East Indies. Between 1571 and 1573 Goiti explored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampanga">Pampanga</a> and Pangasinan and founded several Spanish settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>. He and other soldiers were granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> for the lands they had conquered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1574 about 6,500 Chinese pirates under Limahong sailed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> and laid siege to the Spanish settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, and Goiti was killed by them. His second in command, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-salcedo">Juan de Salcedo</a>, hurried from Ilocos Sur, drove the pirates out of Manila and in 1575 besieged them for three months in Pangasinan, where they surrendered. Goiti's remains lie in a knight's tomb inside the San Agustin Church in Intramuros.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martín de Goiti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1570 a Spanish force under <b>Martin de Goiti</b> burned the town after a clash with Sulayman.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-maynila">Kingdom of Maynila</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. Died in Spanish East Indies, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.58,121&amp;year=1564" aria-label="Spanish East Indies on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Martín de Goiti"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5083 3766h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5083 3766h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=14.58,121&amp;year=1564">What was here in 1564? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
